(
    version: 1,
    id: "m05",
    title: "Mind the Wall",
    chapter: 2,
    concepts: ["Range sensors", "Blind spots"],
    world: (id: "walled_arena", variant: Some("straight"), seed_variants: [(3, "angled")]),
    robot: Starter("rover_tof"),
    time_limit_s: 15.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.7, 1.0),
    brief_md: r#"
## Stop 100 mm from the wall

Drive towards the wall and stop with the **front of the Rover 100 mm from it** (90 to 110 mm), without
touching it. On the third test run the wall is turned by 50 degrees.

This Rover has two distance sensors: the ultrasonic `eye`, which sends a cone of sound, and the
time-of-flight sensor `tof`, which sends narrow beams of light. A flat surface that is turned too far
away from a sensor reflects the signal off to the side, so the sensor sees nothing. Which of the two
sensors still sees a wall that is turned by 50 degrees?
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "The wall is turned 50 degrees away from the ultrasonic sensor. Does the sensor see it? Answer 1 for yes and 0 for no.",
        unit: "1 or 0",
        measured: "sees_angled_wall",
    )),
    criteria: [
        FrontClearance(min_m: 0.09, max_m: 0.11, within_s: 15.0),
        NoContact(tag: "wall"),
    ],
    stars: (
        (text: "Stop within 3 mm of 100 mm", criteria: [FrontClearance(min_m: 0.097, max_m: 0.103, within_s: 15.0)]),
        (text: "Be stopped in under 5 seconds", criteria: [FrontClearance(min_m: 0.097, max_m: 0.103, within_s: 5.0)]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Print the readings of both sensors on the third run. A distance of -1 means the sensor got no echo.",
        "The time-of-flight sensor reads surfaces up to 75 degrees away from its beam; the ultrasonic one only up to 40 degrees.",
        "Drive fast while the wall is far, and slow down as the distance gets small: power = min(0.6, (distance - 0.1) * 2). The sensor reports the distance from itself, so subtract how far the sensor is behind the front of the plate.",
    ),
)
